{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"08","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"Future Learning Plan","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on future learning planning, centered on a future learning plan, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Convert a vague learning intention into a measurable, dated goal tied to a named proof artifact.","Sequence learning goals by prerequisite so each goal builds on a verified earlier skill.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","cat100_supplement_future_learning_plan","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/08/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_calibration"]},"content":"# Future Learning Plan\n\n![Future Learning Plan textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-08-future-learning-plan.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"Get better at coding and learn more about data\" is the kind of goal that feels good to write and is impossible to act on. It has no finish line, no proof, and no sequence, so a month later you cannot tell whether you made progress. The skill this final section builds is turning vague intentions into a learning plan you can actually check: measurable goals, each tied to a concrete deliverable, scheduled with dates, and ordered so that prerequisites come first.\n\nThis matters because the course is ending and no one will assign you the next step. A plan that names a specific artifact (\"a budget file using `SUMIFS` and `VLOOKUP` across 200 rows, posted as a link\") is something you, or an advisor three months from now, can verify happened. A plan that says \"improve at Excel\" cannot be checked by anyone, including you. Learning to write goals with built-in proof is what lets self-directed learning continue after the course stops directing it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Convert a vague learning intention into a measurable, dated goal tied to a named proof artifact.\n- Sequence learning goals by prerequisite so each goal builds on a verified earlier skill.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nRobin finishes CAT 100 and writes a future plan in one line: \"Get better at coding and learn more about data.\" It feels ambitious and motivated. Two months later, Robin cannot answer a simple question: did it happen? There was no target to hit, no artifact to point to, and no order to follow, so \"better\" stayed undefined.\n\nThe intention was real; the plan was unusable. A goal with no measure cannot succeed or fail, which means it cannot guide a single afternoon of effort. The future learning plan turns Robin's wish into rows that each name a goal, the deliverable that proves it, a date, and the prerequisite that must come first, so progress becomes something you can check rather than feel.\n\n## Core Concept\n\n**The three moves below are each evidence-backed.** Specific, difficult goals outperform vague \"do your best\" intentions (Locke & Latham, 2002); attaching a concrete if-then plan for when and where you will act raises follow-through (Gollwitzer's implementation intentions); and sequencing learning so each task builds on verified prerequisite skill is the core of modern instructional-design models such as 4C/ID (van Merrienboer & Sweller).\n\nA future learning plan is a table where each goal carries four parts that together make it verifiable and sequenced:\n\n- **Measurable goal.** A capability stated so completion is unambiguous. \"Master spreadsheet formulas\" is too soft; \"build a budget file using `SUMIFS` and `VLOOKUP` across 200 rows\" has a clear done state.\n- **Proof artifact.** The deliverable that demonstrates the goal, such as a posted link or a screenshot. The proof is what makes the goal checkable by someone other than you.\n- **Target date.** A deadline that turns \"someday\" into a commitment you can fall behind on and notice.\n- **Prerequisite.** What must be learned first, so the plan respects dependencies instead of attempting everything at once.\n\nThe intellectual core is two ideas from goal-setting and skill-building. The first is the difference between an outcome you can measure and a vibe you cannot: a goal that produces an inspectable artifact can be confirmed, while \"get better\" cannot. The second is sequencing by prerequisite. You cannot meaningfully write a database query before you can summarize data in a spreadsheet, so the plan orders the database goal after the spreadsheet goal rather than starting both at once. A plan that ignores prerequisites collapses under its own ambition; a sequenced plan moves one verifiable step at a time.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A goal reads Get better at coding and learn more about data. Why can no one, including you, check it later?\" explanation=\"The goal has no done state, no proof artifact, and no order. The fix is to name the smallest deliverable that would prove progress, such as one working SELECT ... WHERE query with a screenshot, and attach a date and prerequisite.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It has no measurable proof, date, or prerequisite, so progress cannot be confirmed.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is fine; ambition is what matters most in a learning plan.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It fails only because it does not mention a specific programming language.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: From a Vague Wish to a Verifiable Learning Plan\n\nA student finishing CAT 100 first writes a vague plan.\n\n**Before (no measure, no date, no proof).**\n\n> Get better at coding and learn more about data.\n\nThere is no finish line, so progress can never be checked.\n\n**After (measurable goals, sequenced, each with a proof).**\n\n| Goal | Measurable proof | Target date | Prerequisite |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Master spreadsheet formulas | A budget file using `SUMIFS` and `VLOOKUP` across 200 rows, posted as a link | June 15 | Finish CAT 100 Excel unit |\n| Publish a portfolio site | 3 pages live on GitHub Pages with valid HTML headings and a working nav | August 1 | Finish CAT 100 HTML unit |\n| Try a database query | One `SELECT ... WHERE` query that filters the budget data, with a screenshot | September 1 | Spreadsheet goal complete first |\n\nEach row names a goal, a concrete deliverable that proves it, a date, and the prerequisite that must come first. The database goal is sequenced after spreadsheets rather than attempted all at once. A future advisor, or the student in three months, can check each milestone against its proof instead of guessing whether progress happened.\n\nBuild one goal row as a fixed procedure. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Turn a vague intention into a verifiable goal row\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You start with get better at spreadsheets. What question converts it into a checkable goal?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"How motivated do I feel about it\",\"What would I be able to show if I succeeded\",\"How many hours should I spend\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Ask what you could show. That converts get better at spreadsheets into a budget file using SUMIFS and VLOOKUP across 200 rows, posted as a link.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Demand a proof artifact: the link, file, or screenshot that would prove completion. No nameable proof means the goal is still too vague.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Set a target date so someday becomes a commitment you can fall behind on and notice.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Order by prerequisite. Place the database goal after the spreadsheet goal, because querying data assumes you can already structure and summarize it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the steps that build one provable, sequenced goal:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Ask what you could show if the goal succeeded\",\"Restate the goal as a measurable deliverable\",\"Name the proof artifact (link, file, or screenshot)\",\"Attach a concrete target date\",\"Place the goal after any prerequisite it depends on\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_future_learning_plan\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite only goals that produce a deliverable you could show someone, in an order their prerequisites allow. For each goal:\n\n1. **Demand a proof artifact.** If you cannot name the file, link, or screenshot that would prove completion, the goal is too vague to keep.\n2. **Set a date.** A goal without a deadline never becomes urgent; assign one you can be measured against.\n3. **Order by prerequisite.** Place each goal after whatever it depends on, so you build one verifiable step at a time instead of attempting everything at once.\n\nIf a goal has no measurable proof, it is a wish; rewrite it until it produces something checkable.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is mistaking ambition for a plan. \"Learn data science\" is a direction, not a goal; it has no done state and no proof. The fix is to name the smallest deliverable that would show real progress, like one working `SELECT ... WHERE` query, and start there.\n\nThe second misreading is ignoring prerequisites and scheduling everything for next week. Stacking a database goal in the same window as the spreadsheet goal it depends on guarantees both stall. Sequencing is not pessimism; it is what makes each step achievable, because the earlier skill is the foundation the later one stands on.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart by taking a vague intention and asking, \"What would I be able to show if I succeeded?\" That question converts \"get better at spreadsheets\" into \"a budget file using `SUMIFS` and `VLOOKUP` across 200 rows, posted as a link.\" The deliverable is now the goal, and it is checkable.\n\nNext, attach a date and a prerequisite to each goal. The date forces commitment; the prerequisite reveals order. The portfolio-site goal depends on finishing the HTML unit, and the database goal depends on the spreadsheet goal being done first, because querying data assumes you can already structure and summarize it. Lay the goals out so each one sits after what it needs.\n\nFinally, sanity-check the sequence by reading it top to bottom: could you actually complete row two before row three begins? If a later goal secretly depends on an earlier one you scheduled after it, reorder. The walkthrough output is a goal table where every row has a proof, a date, and a prerequisite, and the order respects dependencies.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, draft one goal of your own all the way to its proof artifact and prerequisite. A strong reader does not just admire the example table; they can take a real ambition and pin a checkable deliverable and a correct prerequisite to it.\n\nA strong reader of the future learning plan can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each goal, what specific deliverable would prove it is done?\n2. What date is attached, and is it concrete enough to fall behind on?\n3. What must be learned first, and does the order respect that prerequisite?\n4. Where a goal is still vague, what is the smallest provable version of it?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe plan is strong when every goal names a measurable proof artifact, carries a date, and sits in an order its prerequisites justify. The standard is not a long list of aspirations; a three-goal plan where each goal is checkable and correctly sequenced beats a sprawling wish list with no proofs.\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"I rewrote 'get better at coding and data' into three goals, each with a deliverable I can post: a 200-row `SUMIFS` and `VLOOKUP` budget file by June 15, a three-page GitHub Pages site by August 1, and one `SELECT ... WHERE` query with a screenshot by September 1. I sequenced the database goal after the spreadsheet goal because querying data assumes I can already structure and summarize it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nApply the plan to a non-technical goal, such as preparing for an internship search. The structure holds: \"get a good internship\" becomes measurable goals like \"complete three tailored applications by July 1,\" each with a proof (the submitted application) and a prerequisite (a finished resume and portfolio, which themselves come from earlier sections of this chapter). Writing a sequenced, provable plan for a goal that has no formula or code at all is the test of whether you have internalized goal-setting as a reusable method rather than a spreadsheet-specific exercise.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the future learning plan packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-08-future-learning-plan-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"future learning plan\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the future learning plan.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\nIf you draft the plan itself with AI, keep the move bounded and current: as of 2026, a general-purpose assistant such as ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini can draft a goal table you then critique row by row, and structured course catalogs can supply the sequenced prerequisites a chat draft often misses. Tools and models change quickly, so verify current versions and your institution's AI-use policy before relying on them. Under the prevailing 2026 disclose-and-approve norm, AI-assisted drafting is acceptable when it is disclosed and instructor-approved, and the row-by-row critique you apply to the draft is the part that is yours.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the future learning plan for CAT 100 08.08. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE Standards for Students](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_future_learning_plan\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the future learning plan records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Locke, E. A., & Latham, G. P. (2002). Building a practically useful theory of goal setting and task motivation. American Psychologist, 57(9). https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.57.9.705\n- [ISTE Standards for Students](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_future_learning_plan","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_future_learning_plan","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"Your future learning plan lists the goal 'Get better at coding.' Why will this goal be hard to act on or check?","options":["It is too short to be a goal.","It has no measurable outcome or deadline, so you cannot tell when it is met; 'Publish a 3-page HTML site by August 1' is checkable.","Coding is not a CAT 100 topic.","Goals should never mention coding."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A usable goal is measurable and dated. 'Get better' has no finish line; a concrete deliverable with a date does.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_vague_goal","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Your plan has the milestone 'Understand Excel formulas.' What turns it into a milestone a reviewer can verify?","options":["Add the word 'really' before 'understand'.","Attach a proof: 'Build a budget file that uses SUMIFS and VLOOKUP across 200 rows and post the file link.'","Move it to the end of the list.","Remove the deadline so there is less pressure."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Each milestone needs an attached deliverable. A finished file using named functions is proof; 'understand Excel' is not checkable.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_no_proof_milestone","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You ask an AI tool for a learning plan and it returns ten advanced topics (databases, machine learning, cloud deploy, cybersecurity, and more) all for next month. What is the disciplined response?","options":["Accept all ten; the AI knows the field better than you.","Reject the unrealistic scope, sequence by prerequisite, and pick one solid next step such as mastering spreadsheet formulas before databases.","Do the topics in alphabetical order.","Drop the plan because it is too hard."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"You judge the AI proposal against realism and prerequisites. A sequenced, achievable next step beats ten unordered advanced topics.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_unrealistic_scope","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_future_learning_plan_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which future-learning-plan row is specific, measurable, dated, and tied to proof?","options":["'Learn data stuff someday.'","'Goal: publish a portfolio site. Proof: 3 pages live on GitHub Pages with valid HTML. Date: Aug 1. Prereq: finish CAT 100 HTML unit first.'","'Be good at computers.'","'Improve over time.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong row names the goal, the measurable proof, the date, and the prerequisite, so progress can be tracked and verified.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_no_proof_milestone","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_vague_goal","pattern":"goal_without_measure","description":"Writing a learning goal that has no measurable outcome or deadline, so progress cannot be checked","trigger":"plan says 'get better at coding' or 'learn more about data'","feedback":"Make goals measurable and dated. 'Build and publish a 3-page portfolio site by August 1' can be checked; 'get better at coding' cannot.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_no_proof_milestone","pattern":"milestone_without_evidence","description":"Listing milestones with no artifact or proof that would show the milestone was reached","trigger":"milestone is 'understand Excel' with no deliverable attached","feedback":"Attach a proof to each milestone, such as a finished pivot-table file or a live page URL, so a future reviewer or you can confirm it happened.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_8_unrealistic_scope","pattern":"all_at_once_no_sequence","description":"Planning to learn many large skills simultaneously with no sequence or prerequisite order","trigger":"plan lists ten advanced topics for one month with no ordering","feedback":"Sequence by prerequisite and pick a realistic next step. Master spreadsheet formulas before databases; one solid, sequenced goal beats ten unordered ones.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d4469b9b18267e0512e42c011a4f542fac88c47b3e74b4db940b18723787ee77","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}